uuid "f4a2491f-cde1-5b66-bf41-f5ac5ec2940b" :question/problem "![](images/ab/ab6a640488e5b20fa205260aeb31fbcf6c49428087b2a62277a9e58118f3e850.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=5$ and the vertical radius has length $b=7.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643349 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34d65ba7-2d47-58e9-98df-d1268e0787d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643334}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643334} :answer-field/id #uuid "a574c658-09ca-56e2-a090-d347e8187c31" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fc3d00aa-46fe-581e-ac5c-06686ed74c14" :question/problem "![](images/4f/4f2a5176bc5674bf5d755e0659aac28d7a2f9d2c7bf6af08c49a3f38658a6364.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=2$ and the vertical radius has length $b=7.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643454 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b6e07fa-871c-572f-9953-675d2d30b6c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{51}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643467}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643467} :answer-field/id #uuid "2141d16d-dd50-5459-9dfc-6d140670aef4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643459} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89b81b87-fe8c-5604-9af6-783282a55dad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643465}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643465} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2414124-5688-5fda-860d-53d2990e2917" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "002152e8-5b3c-5d0e-80a6-c79a1d4dfd75" :question/problem "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{51}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{20}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=51$ and ${b}^{2}=20.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=51,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{51}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=20,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=\\sqrt{51},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{51}=2\\sqrt{51},$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{5}=4\\sqrt{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643456 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17bf4454-fc58-5647-9832-9af090145b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{94}$ and $2\\sqrt{83}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643457} {:answer/id #uuid "244c3a91-3a19-51ab-b47b-25a2b93ae66f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{22}$ and $4\\sqrt{14}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643460} {:answer/id #uuid "bc933607-05e2-5404-a7f2-c2b4df14089a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{21}$ and $4\\sqrt{7}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643470} {:answer/id #uuid "cb55091f-ee35-53a9-992c-cc2ae6250ebd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{19}$ and $2\\sqrt{57}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643474} {:answer/id #uuid "d53def7f-7dc6-56ed-a5a9-760c3d67e1fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{10}$ and $8\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643477}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643460} :answer-field/id #uuid "db4b2297-162c-525a-aef6-b5874f51035c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1569191d-f868-5104-8548-6bb62bc99b93" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{88}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{56}=1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=88$ and ${b}^{2}=56.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=88,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{88}=2\\sqrt{22}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=56,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{56}=2\\sqrt{14}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=2\\sqrt{22},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{14}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{22}=4\\sqrt{22},$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{14}=4\\sqrt{14}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adb95e24-eabc-57f9-84e7-c15fd5f1b597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643469}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643469} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf5848e1-7c80-5bbd-b0f1-ac9a7532aebc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643471} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0232bd3e-4933-570c-a5fe-4788a09875f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643455}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643455} :answer-field/id #uuid "df7ff49a-ce3d-5e5e-9f7a-473613492fa1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5f0bfeea-fcdf-5d3e-9275-beec38209c9a" :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{30}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=30$ and ${b}^{2}=2.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=30,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{30}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=2,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=\\sqrt{30},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{30}=2\\sqrt{30},$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}=2\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643478 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "818dea69-4bfb-56b5-aa32-71c3f92ded37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$14$ and $12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643464} {:answer/id #uuid "c41cc4f0-dc39-5198-8966-4b7bb9f16198" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$12$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643472} {:answer/id #uuid "d380967b-4ac8-5a06-a099-b6f7e56cd31c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$10$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643476} {:answer/id #uuid "dc50e36b-9a3b-5415-bc87-5b35b6b5516e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$16$ and $10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643480} {:answer/id #uuid "e770468c-74e3-5b59-a382-274ede177b4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$20$ and $14$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643483}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643483} :answer-field/id #uuid "97f5a0f0-17d0-5275-a79e-b64dd7eded9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d8ff0d51-8878-558a-b310-4862399de7ad" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{100}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=100$ and ${b}^{2}=49.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=100,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{100}=10.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=49,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{49}=7.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=10,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=7.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 10=20,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 7=14.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18aa1992-3ffe-5540-b1ec-3f749521ec71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{97}$ and $4\\sqrt{17}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643458} {:answer/id #uuid "28a1b703-d1f1-5f25-a3bb-70baff366a0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{79}$ and $6\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643461} {:answer/id #uuid "6a236c0c-47c5-56b0-818d-9dc761ef4b2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{61}$ and $2\\sqrt{33}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643463} {:answer/id #uuid "c49d426c-d57a-598c-80f7-ad6cafd3180d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$6\\sqrt{11}$ and $2\\sqrt{35}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643473} {:answer/id #uuid "cce169c9-efd6-5518-aef9-184e65503038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{85}$ and $2\\sqrt{78}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643475}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643463} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c534e9e-9262-5691-af1c-40c3f470e0c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fcf6df82-9cf5-5ccd-974d-0b60b763b971" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{33}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{61}=1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=33$ and ${b}^{2}=61.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=33,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{33}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=61,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{61}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=\\sqrt{61},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\sqrt{33}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{61}=2\\sqrt{61},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{33}=2\\sqrt{33}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186647019 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3693d855-ebab-5996-a89e-ea0bdce6aa0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-15y(y-980),\\,y(0)=1\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646519} {:answer/id #uuid "473824f5-8066-5079-82b9-d15a24e4782e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-15(y-980),\\,y(0)=1\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646540} {:answer/id #uuid "4c233e47-6194-5061-9305-0068276f8873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=15(y-980),\\,y(0)=1\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646553} {:answer/id #uuid "e3bea445-a5b5-558d-b5d4-4e3f5335ec5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-15(y-980),\\,y(0)=800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646839} {:answer/id #uuid "f95faf65-d568-575b-8ebd-319a9e5ed303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=15y(y-980),\\,y(0)=800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646879}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186646540} :answer-field/id #uuid "6aac2a9f-cccd-510f-8c5b-c6bfbd25a6ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "18875c41-9995-5408-b5e4-f2b21291bc3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-106660" :question/problem "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited decay problem?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-15(y-980),\\,y(0)=1\\,000.\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=15,a=980,$ and ${y}_{0}=1\\,000.$ Notice that ${y}_{0}>a,$ which is required for inhibited decay. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited decay. In particular, the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-15(y-980),\\,y(0)=800,\n$$\n\ndoes *not* represent inhibited decay model because ${y}_{0}=800<a=980.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186647026 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "133fb08e-6e11-58a8-81ee-87a18e4cd564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=kT(T-70)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646459} {:answer/id #uuid "5e81fb71-2df7-520f-99b1-a5e61135988b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-kT(T-70)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646591} {:answer/id #uuid "5e9369f0-48a5-58bc-9836-0903071b2780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T-70)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646592} {:answer/id #uuid "90b6e089-03cb-5773-a181-66b484da898c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T+70)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646682} {:answer/id #uuid "baa23692-87a3-5060-a06e-eb5c154e276e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=k(T-70)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646762}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186646592} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b42d274-2892-572d-98ad-26961422bfe9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "29a46a1b-3fea-5ef3-98e8-a12878014c94" :question/math-academy-id "q-106877" :question/problem "The rate at which a freshly baked cake cools is proportional to the difference between its temperature and the temperature of its surroundings. Let $T(t)$ be the temperature of the cake in degrees Fahrenheit, and let $t>0$ be the time in minutes. If the temperature of the surroundings is ${70}^{∘}\\text{F},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the cake? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={70}^{∘}\\text{F},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-70).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186647040 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0567bc3f-9df8-555c-9eb4-4877b8ad08f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "31\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646436} {:answer/id #uuid "47771db8-78e6-59e2-b53e-aae2adeb3b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "45\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646541} {:answer/id #uuid "658304df-2c24-562b-ba9c-46810e0ee683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "37\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646609} {:answer/id #uuid "6dfdadce-247d-59d5-bebf-ac00fdbc2cab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "29\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646623} {:answer/id #uuid "fe828fde-8fe3-568c-b775-f98fa439bbf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "28\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646888}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186646609} :answer-field/id #uuid "d35bfdda-4adb-54fe-b087-fcfe2627f7e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646989}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4360bcb1-959b-53b1-855f-91f5d582be11" :question/math-academy-id "q-107123" :question/problem "A pork leg is taken out of the oven with a temperature of ${150}^{∘}C,$ and placed in a room that maintains a temperature of ${24}^{∘}C$, and its temperature begins to decrease. After $15$ minutes, the  temperature of the pork leg is ${100}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the pork leg to have a temperature of ${60}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the pork leg decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the pork leg was transferred into the ${24}^{∘}C$ room.*" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={24}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-24).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-24} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-24} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-24| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-24>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-24 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-24 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-24 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =24+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the pork leg was ${150}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=150,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}150 & =24+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 150 & =24+K \\\\ K & =126\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=24+126{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $15$ minutes, the temperature of the pork leg is ${100}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(15)=100,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =24+126{e}^{-15k} \\\\ 76 & =126{e}^{-15k} \\\\ \\frac{38}{63} & ={e}^{-15k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{38}{63}\\right) & =-15k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{15}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{38}{63}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the pork leg has a temperature of ${60}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =60 \\\\ 24+126{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =60 \\\\ 126{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =36 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the pork leg will have a temperature of ${60}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right)}{-\\frac{1}{15}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{38}{63}\\right)}≈37\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186647042 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17f9765f-cae1-5047-89d8-11e4a7dfe9cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{28}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646464} {:answer/id #uuid "3403c6e3-ec8a-53c5-a425-cc33f931d370" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{34}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646512} {:answer/id #uuid "34661e1b-3a93-50b1-ae0f-ea69ac80e885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646514} {:answer/id #uuid "3d3f2cd0-e2b5-5544-8182-2ed4047d9fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{32}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646528} {:answer/id #uuid "be5ba072-dbbf-558e-8149-4e7211637e48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{36}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646767}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186646767} :answer-field/id #uuid "e33ab9c8-fcf0-5f44-afca-e7fe6606a08c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186646996}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "44b64320-501f-5d6a-b8c7-b8cf6a960fc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-107121" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a body can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.06(T-21)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the body is ${40}^{∘}C,$ calculate the temperature of the body after $4$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the body is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.06(T-21).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-21} & =-0.06\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-21} & =-\\int 0.06\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-21| & =-0.06t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-21>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-21 & ={e}^{-0.06t+C} \\\\ T-21 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.06t} \\\\ T-21 & =K{e}^{-0.06t} \\\\ T & =21+K{e}^{-0.06t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the body was ${40}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=40,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40 & =21+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 40 & =21+K \\\\ K & =19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=21+19{e}^{-0.06t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the body after $t=4$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(4)=21+19{e}^{-0.06\\cdot 4}≈{36}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186650926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b1a238-44d3-5180-8740-255843d424b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{yx-{x}^{2}}{y+x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650456} {:answer/id #uuid "6b70300d-77a3-5391-a17e-f2bb289f8b4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{y-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650609} {:answer/id #uuid "7a493e10-27fb-5525-821d-bc2ac8872d18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650639} {:answer/id #uuid "ea28b693-b14c-54a3-a3fb-894ca4f6c3af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y-x}{1+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650812} {:answer/id #uuid "f0ee6eb7-157c-5018-8fcd-6a70bbbc940e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{yx-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+xy}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650822}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186650822} :answer-field/id #uuid "19f24883-8782-5fd1-a7d3-05eb46f3260d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "088cf836-9b71-5a51-a96a-13c950d7db07" :question/math-academy-id "q-14317" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is the case is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{yx-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+xy}.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=\\frac{yx-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+xy}.$\n\nWe see that both the numerator and denominator are homogeneous polynomials of degree $2.$ Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree $2-2=0.$\n\nHence, the original differential equation is homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{yx-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+xy}\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)(tx)-(tx)^{2}}{(ty)^{2}+(tx)(ty)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}xy-{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{t}^{2}{y}^{2}+{t}^{2}xy} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{{t}^{2}(xy-{x}^{2})}{{t}^{2}({y}^{2}+xy)} \\\\  & =\\frac{xy-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+xy} \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ the original differential equation is homogeneous."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186650949 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49b77d66-e568-5be8-bd8c-edc436ed3ea1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650559} {:answer/id #uuid "5f537ba9-25aa-51ff-8064-7a9ae8e74a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-2y}{y}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650589} {:answer/id #uuid "da8fe273-e8eb-54a2-afa7-ca7d453b7013" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650788} {:answer/id #uuid "ec97c673-6040-53e6-a4f7-b3b96109620d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650817} {:answer/id #uuid "f6d26905-999c-5c66-8a33-f402e0c618e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{xy}{x-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650828}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186650828} :answer-field/id #uuid "831dde12-2282-5a0c-b736-dada8dc73459" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "55464993-be5a-5fb3-ae2e-5285e38aaed6" :question/math-academy-id "q-90598" :question/problem "Which of the following is NOT a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is NOT the case is:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{xy}{x-y}\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=\\frac{xy}{x-y}.$\n\nWe see that the numerator is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $2$ but the denominator is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $1.$  Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree $2-1=1.$\n\nHence, the original differential equation is  NOT homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{xy}{x-y}\\,\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is not homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(tx)(ty)}{tx-ty} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}xy}{t(x-y)} \\\\  & =t\\cdot \\frac{xy}{x-y} \\\\  & =tf(x,y)\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ the original differential equation is NOT homogeneous."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186650967 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31b7acbc-ba6a-5373-90f8-1fb1bd746430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650530} {:answer/id #uuid "387b9173-140e-51ee-b3e6-e1dd1f2434a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650537} {:answer/id #uuid "83b435d0-9f48-5153-bb92-4c0a31c49b2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650660} {:answer/id #uuid "90886ce9-1908-561a-8fdd-59f0e5653d7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650681} {:answer/id #uuid "b4bc2ca8-f09c-5b57-a98e-525c2e2afc32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650735}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186650530} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ba406f9-2b25-5984-adac-d96cc5a5c6b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "854d0d8e-36ce-595c-94d3-8225f1bb08f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-14320" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}+yx+{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This is a homogeneous equation because it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y),\n$$\n\nand we notice that all terms in the numerator and denominator of $f(x,y)$ are of order $2$ (i.e., they are quadratic).\n\nUsing the transformation $y=v(x)x$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}+yx+{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{(vx)^{2}+(vx)x+{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{x}^{2}({v}^{2}+v+1)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & ={v}^{2}+v+1-v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & ={v}^{2}+1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{v}^{2}+1}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{v}^{2}+1}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{v}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}v & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(v) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ v & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above into our solution, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C) \\\\ y & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186650988 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60e62c85-c566-5596-aeac-6a35161edf2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{v}{{v}^{2}+3}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650593} {:answer/id #uuid "7027b7c0-7c21-5d8c-9fe2-36b2b2a2c598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650618} {:answer/id #uuid "a034791d-9f5d-5ee2-9fc6-27b076b86a88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{v}{v+1}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650697} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c23df8-2c98-54d9-bc30-2be4b43ef609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{v+1}{3{v}^{2}}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650797} {:answer/id #uuid "e9aba5bb-d415-5654-ae17-def064e5f000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650810}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186650618} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1bf3348-64f3-5a21-8d0a-ec72df9b6d5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186650914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e1385b74-52ff-53cd-91df-70b0f0ad6473" :question/math-academy-id "q-90600" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}}{xy}.\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}}{xy} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{(vx)^{2}+3{x}^{2}}{x(vx)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{x}^{2}({v}^{2}+3)}{{x}^{2}v} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}({v}^{2}+3)}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}v} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{v}^{2}+3}{v} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =v+\\frac{3}{v} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3}{v} \\\\ v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657635 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "841e2942-bfa2-5b85-b44b-5fcecbbf51ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 6 \\cdot 6^{- 2 x^{3} - 4} x^{2} \\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657644}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657644} :answer-field/id #uuid "05032cba-eb6d-54e4-9bd4-90a4529fda64" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657633}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0b8b61b6-6058-58ee-b489-5e87a3738e1a" :question/problem "If $f(x)={6}^{-4-2{x}^{3}}$, find ${f}^{\\prime}(x)$.\n\n$f^{\\prime}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={6}^{u},\\,u=-4-2{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}={6}^{u}\\ln(6),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-6{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\\\ & ={6}^{u}\\ln(6)\\cdot (-6{x}^{2}) \\\\ & =(-6{x}^{2})\\cdot {6}^{u}\\ln(6) \\\\ & =-6{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{-4-2{x}^{3}}\\ln(6).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657636 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c1c75b8-e26c-5124-9d8e-2b0c7d538071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4 \\cdot 2^{- 3 x^{3} + 2 x} \\left(2 - 9 x^{2}\\right) \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657638}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657638} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad146e83-d809-55d5-b5d2-7a4807735ed5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "11669aef-a5f9-5388-a104-0a436ef45585" :question/problem "Given that $y=4\\cdot {2}^{2x-3{x}^{3}}$, find $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$.\n\n$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=4\\cdot {2}^{u},\\,u=2x-3{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}=4\\cdot {2}^{u}\\ln(2),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=2-9{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\\\ & =4\\cdot {2}^{u}\\ln(2)\\cdot (2-9{x}^{2}) \\\\ & =(2-9{x}^{2})\\cdot 4\\cdot {2}^{u}\\ln(2) \\\\ & =(8-36{x}^{2})\\cdot {2}^{2x-3{x}^{3}}\\ln(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657637 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e0a109a-1398-5203-b687-1fca003844e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-48\\cdot 2^{3 - 9 x}\\ln(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657639} {:answer/id #uuid "75a0a275-f824-5f1a-878e-960f624e19ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-85\\cdot 2^{3 - 9 x}\\ln(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657642} {:answer/id #uuid "7af2cbb1-148f-5a29-b908-4002f80f58ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "85\\cdot 2^{3 - 9 x}\\ln(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657643} {:answer/id #uuid "aa1a8145-bf09-5948-ab9e-9a0d45e521a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36\\cdot 2^{3 - 9 x}\\ln(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657647} {:answer/id #uuid "dad54a83-3367-58e2-9a9d-cba745b58143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-36\\cdot 2^{3 - 9 x}\\ln(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657653}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657653} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1dcd53c-c98c-5ecf-b693-90aedb451257" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "28d91041-6f71-527c-b0ba-ad8e852f62c1" :question/problem "If $f(x)=4\\cdot {2}^{3-9x}$, find ${f}^{\\prime}(x)$." :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=4\\cdot {2}^{u},\\,u=3-9x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}=4\\cdot {2}^{u}\\ln(2),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-9.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\\\ & =4\\cdot {2}^{u}\\ln(2)\\cdot (-9) \\\\ & =(-9)\\cdot 4\\cdot {2}^{u}\\ln(2) \\\\ & =-36\\cdot {2}^{3-9x}\\ln(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657640 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0904557f-31ec-5d61-be02-e94f759e9131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\cdot 3^{- 4 x}\\ln(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657634} {:answer/id #uuid "92a466b7-1343-5de6-93ed-81dc8eaae4cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9\\cdot 3^{- 4 x}\\ln(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657645} {:answer/id #uuid "9fb0deac-7b1c-574a-8c8d-c13074ff7589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\cdot 3^{- 4 x}\\ln(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657646} {:answer/id #uuid "be75eec8-65d0-5bd5-a24a-080e621f031b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6\\cdot 3^{- 4 x}\\ln(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657649} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f64b24-5080-5a4a-b39c-7931a40f9db8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\cdot 3^{- 4 x}\\ln(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657650}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657646} :answer-field/id #uuid "70c563c1-28fa-57f6-9e2c-777ad735321f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6f7f3334-06bf-5b6c-8d9b-7e1b11d303db" :question/problem "Given that $y={3}^{-4x}$, find $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={3}^{u},\\,u=-4x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}={3}^{u}\\ln(3),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\\\ & ={3}^{u}\\ln(3)\\cdot (-4) \\\\ & =(-4)\\cdot {3}^{u}\\ln(3) \\\\ & =-4\\cdot {3}^{-4x}\\ln(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657654 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2c2af5a-8964-54fb-89be-8cfcc69077a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2 \\cdot 6^{x^{3} + 3 x} \\left(3 x^{2} + 3\\right) \\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657655}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657655} :answer-field/id #uuid "dabf61a3-2a66-5d43-8d1a-277898ddbf85" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "df3a7a3e-f0f6-5faf-b140-d8c0078a9bd5" :question/problem "Given that $y=2\\cdot {6}^{3x+{x}^{3}}$, find $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$.\n\n$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=2\\cdot {6}^{u},\\,u=3x+{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}=2\\cdot {6}^{u}\\ln(6),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=3+3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\\\ & =2\\cdot {6}^{u}\\ln(6)\\cdot (3+3{x}^{2}) \\\\ & =(3+3{x}^{2})\\cdot 2\\cdot {6}^{u}\\ln(6) \\\\ & =(6+6{x}^{2})\\cdot {6}^{3x+{x}^{3}}\\ln(6).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657674 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5821db7a-52c1-5685-ac1f-a24dea525453" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{4x+3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657666} {:answer/id #uuid "896f4fb3-780c-5394-a7c4-2c48f10ada9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{4x+3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657673} {:answer/id #uuid "b3a0983b-55bc-545c-b0a0-d6ac4873d0e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4x+3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657678} {:answer/id #uuid "e78062d7-2943-58e7-8f8a-56e43f8a7b3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4x+3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657681} {:answer/id #uuid "ff390e11-93f5-5e2b-b5a0-81c8b17df467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4x+3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657683}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657683} :answer-field/id #uuid "74026f16-a351-5b82-a094-4d783b5f71be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657670}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "90db644a-fd4a-5c58-b43a-e0452a6336d1" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt{4x+3},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=4x+3.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}={u}^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (4) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4x+3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657676 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73f4d5cf-a461-500b-bfff-59db9cb0a932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2x-6)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657669}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657669} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d327192-03f4-5c34-804d-23703a9d60c8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657661}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "99c047a7-758b-5db9-9256-347c7b790ef5" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-6}}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2x-6.$ Then $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}={u}^{-1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2x-6)^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657677 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3441c0fe-baf2-58ec-be43-0bc6b1dc52bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{(12-2x)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657663} {:answer/id #uuid "38fa11e4-1ab5-5a61-9543-a49af6fcfcde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(12-2x)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657665} {:answer/id #uuid "7194ccdf-1cf5-5662-86a2-61c314c00de0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2(12-2x)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657668} {:answer/id #uuid "8179b0e5-d27d-5436-849a-0b6a81884973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2(12-2x)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657671} {:answer/id #uuid "d8efcb49-91d1-50ec-ad3e-649069f99598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{(12-2x)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657679}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657665} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d105e46-25ee-5153-a38b-ae7c0422f899" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186657660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ab67789f-4616-5914-aa8d-59736a54c4f6" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-2x+12}}.$" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-2x+12.$ Then $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}={u}^{-1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-2x+12{)}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
